World Scientific Network for Technological Transition Analysis (WSNTTA): A Mathematical and Architectural Framework for Civilizational Transition Modeling

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Status: Theoretical PhD Dissertation — Final Version

Field: Complex Systems Science, Technology Governance, Applied Mathematics

---

Abstract

This dissertation develops a mathematically rigorous and architecturally specified framework for observing, modeling, and understanding technological transitions at the intersection of artificial intelligence, robotics, energy systems, advanced manufacturing, biotechnology, and space infrastructure. The central thesis is that the principal institutional challenge of the coming decades is not the absence of organizations studying individual technologies, but the absence of sufficiently integrated mechanisms capable of detecting, measuring, and modeling interactions between rapidly evolving technological domains. We formalize a multidimensional state-space representation of technological transitions, replacing scalar readiness indices with vector-valued maturity profiles. We develop a dynamic coupling estimation framework grounded in transfer entropy, Granger causality, and partial cross-correlation, with explicit treatment of directedness, time lags, identifiability conditions, and the binding constraint of data availability for civilizational indicators. We propose a four-channel early-warning architecture adapted from the Karimov–Alekberli framework, positioned as a candidate subject to comparative validation under matched false-alarm protocols rather than an established system. We specify a federated, privacy-preserving computational architecture for proprietary telemetry exchange with quantified privacy–utility trade-offs, incorporating the empirically demonstrated necessity of combining differential privacy with stochastic privacy modules. We design a distributed institutional architecture—Scientific Core, Domain Nodes, Observatories, Mission Cells, and Global Research Network—governed by principles of coordination without centralization, evidence separated from decision, and adversarial Blue/Red Team dynamics, drawing on the African Economic Research Consortium precedent for scientific independence under diversified funding. We conclude with a pre-registered validation protocol based on retrospective transition studies, delay comparison, and independent replication. The dissertation contributes not a finished instrument but a falsifiable research program with explicit success and failure criteria.

Keywords: technological transitions, complex systems, composite indicators, early warning signals, coupling estimation, federated learning, institutional design, civilizational readiness.

