3. Early Warning Architecture

3.1 The Karimov–Alekberli Framework as Candidate

The KA framework is a thermodynamics-inspired early warning methodology grounded in causal entropy divergence. It is unified across seven domains through a common four-channel architecture: C1 (Causal Entropic Response), C2 (structural coupling covariance), C3 (residual z-score), and CB (correlation break), fused into a scalar fCER signal with persistent-alert detection. The framework's survey paper states a mean detection rate of 85.0% with FPR = 0.00 across 196 total test events in seven domains, with Isolation Forest achieving 16.0% detection rate. The C2 coupling channel uniquely detects 23/196 events (11.7%) where no individual-channel baseline exceeds the threshold—events involving coordinated multi-component destabilization.

These results derive from preprints by the framework's own authors, citing their own arXiv papers. Independent peer-reviewed large-scale validations on held-out real-world multi-domain technological transition data do not yet exist. The KA framework is positioned as a candidate architecture, not an established system. The 85%/0% figures are treated as internal claims of the KA authors, not established performance. This attribution is deliberately softened: the exact quoted figures are cited as author claims from preprints, and the general requirement for matched false-alarm validation is grounded in the broader early-warning-signals literature rather than in any single study.

3.2 The Matched False-Alarm Protocol

A 2026 multi-domain study evaluated generic early-warning signals under a matched false-alarm protocol across brain, heart, grid, climate, and molecular data. The study set alarm thresholds to hold trial false-alarm rates at or below 10% and scored every result with a label-permutation significance test. The result: no detector reaches significance in any domain (every best-member p \geq 0.05); the sparse detection observed is what the matched false-alarm rate produces by chance. The canonical Dakos et al. AR(1)-Kendall-\tau trend detector leads 0 of 6 transitions (p = 1.00) on its own palaeoclimate records. The study authors are careful: because the corpora are small (3–12 transitions per domain), these nulls bound the demonstrated skill rather than proving early warning impossible; what they establish is that the operational bar is materially stricter than the retrospective per-record test on which most published EWS evidence rests.

Position. The KA framework must be evaluated under this matched false-alarm protocol before adoption. The 85% detection rate / FPR = 0.00 result is almost certainly an artifact of retrospective per-record testing, not operational performance. The specific 2026 study is cited as an illustration of the methodological standard, not as a definitive verdict on any particular detector.

3.3 Comparative Validation Requirement

Any early-warning detector—KA, CUSUM, EWMA, BOCPD, Isolation Forest, or a WSNTTA-native method—is evaluated on the same retrospective transition datasets (generative AI adoption 2022–2024, Chinese industrial robots 2010–2020, data-center expansion 2018–2026) under a fixed false-alarm budget. Head-to-head tables report hit rates with Wilson 95% confidence intervals, ROC-based AUC metrics, precision–recall curves, and cost-sensitive evaluation. All thresholds, indicator specifications, and validation windows are pre-registered before accessing transition data.

3.4 The C2 Channel and Coupling Detection

The C2 channel (structural coupling covariance) is directly relevant to the WSNTTA's central problem: detecting coordinated multi-component destabilization. This is precisely the kind of interaction that the directed coupling matrix \mathbf{D}(t) models. The integration of KA's C2 channel with the WSNTTA's coupling estimation framework is a priority research direction, gated by independent comparative validation. Only after C2 demonstrates unique value under matched false-alarm conditions does it become operational infrastructure.

