5. Validation Protocol

5.1 The Four Brutal Questions

Question WSNTTA Response
What is measured? \mathbf{X}(t) = [\mathbf{R}, \mathbf{C}, \mathbf{D}, \mathbf{L}, \mathbf{V}]
How is it normalized? Explicit functions with sensitivity analysis and fixed reference standards
How is coupling inferred? Directed transfer entropy, Granger causality, partial cross-correlation, time-lagged, tiered by network size, with identifiability conditions stated
How is early detection validated? Pre-registered matched false-alarm protocol, retrospective studies, delay comparison, independent replication

5.2 Pre-Registration Commitment

Before accessing any transition data for validation, a timestamped protocol is published specifying:

· the exact indicators and their normalization functions;
· the coupling estimation method and its hyperparameters;
· the alarm threshold and false-alarm budget;
· the success criterion (\Delta\tau > 0 with statistical significance and FPR < 5%);
· the failure criterion (\Delta\tau \leq 0 or unacceptable FPR);
· the exact data sources and their availability dates.

This transforms validation from a retrospective exercise into a genuine out-of-sample test.

5.3 Retrospective Validation

Protocol. Apply \mathcal{R}(t) to documented transitions: generative AI adoption (2022–2024), industrial robot deployment in China (2010–2020), data center expansion (2018–2026).

Metrics.

\Delta\tau = \tau_{\text{baseline}} - \tau_{\text{WSNTTA}}

Success criterion. \Delta\tau > 0 statistically significant across at least three independent transitions, with false positive rate < 5% under matched false-alarm conditions.

Failure criterion. If \Delta\tau \leq 0 or if false positive rate is unacceptable, the architecture is revised or abandoned. At least one independent external group (not the dissertation author or the initial Scientific Core) replicates the first three retrospective cases.

5.4 Failure Mode Analysis

The architecture is judged unsuccessful under the following conditions:

1. Persistent inability to beat baselines on \Delta\tau under matched false-alarm conditions.
2. Capture of funding or governance by a single state, firm, or ideological bloc.
3. Inability to obtain high-quality multi-domain time series of sufficient length and regularity.
4. Systematic failure of the coupling estimator to recover known directed relationships in benchmark tests.
5. Inability to produce Mission Cell outputs that demonstrate unique cross-disciplinary value.

