Appendix C — Normalization and Aggregation Pipeline (Design Specification)

C.1 Purpose

To define, without executing it, the full transformation from raw indicator to maturity sub-index and to composite constructions, including missing-data, zero-value, structural-break, and sensitivity handling.

C.2 Normalization Functions

C.2.1 Clipped min-max robust.

\tilde{x} = \text{clip}\!\left(\frac{x - x_{0.05}}{x_{0.95} - x_{0.05}},\ 0,\ 1\right)
\tag{C.1}

The 5th and 95th percentiles are computed on a frozen reference window \mathcal{R}_0 declared in the pre-registration (Appendix J). The window is never updated retroactively within a study.

C.2.2 Sigmoid normalization.

\tilde{x} = \sigma\!\left(\frac{x-\mu}{s}\right) = \frac{1}{1+e^{-(x-\mu)/s}}
\tag{C.2}

with \mu, s estimated on \mathcal{R}_0 and then frozen. A guard s > \epsilon_s is imposed to prevent degenerate scaling.

C.2.3 Saturating threshold normalization.

\tilde{x} = \min\!\left(1,\ \frac{x}{x_{\text{ref}}}\right)
\tag{C.3}

with x_{\text{ref}} a declared physical or institutional reference value and x_{\text{ref}} > 0.

C.3 Zero-Value and Negative-Value Handling

Let \mathcal{Z} be the set of indicators whose normalized support includes zero. For geometric aggregation we adopt the shifted form

g_i = \exp\!\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=1}^{n} \ln\!\left(1 + \tilde{x}_{ik}\right)\right) - 1
\tag{C.4}

which is defined for \tilde{x}_{ik} \geq 0 and preserves the non-compensatory ordering property in the limit of small scores.

For indicators whose normalized support may become negative under clipping or detrending, the transformation is applied after a shift

\tilde{x}' = \tilde{x} + c_0,\qquad c_0 = \max(0,\ -\min_i \tilde{x}_i) + \epsilon
\tag{C.5}

so that the shifted support is strictly positive before logarithmic transformation.

C.4 Weight Constraints

All weight vectors appearing in Section 2.4 and in this appendix are constrained:

w_i \in [0,1],\qquad \sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i = 1.
\tag{C.6}

Weights are elicited by documented expert judgment (Appendix E for the coupling prior; a parallel protocol for the maturity weights) and are subjected to the Dirichlet sensitivity analysis of C.7. No weight may be negative, and no subset of weights may dominate without explicit justification in the pre-registration.

C.5 Missing-Data Policy

Missingness type Treatment
MCAR Multiple imputation, M=20 replicates, propagated into uncertainty
MAR Model-based imputation conditioned on observed covariates
MNAR Flagged; indicator demoted to candidate; excluded from headline \mathbf{R}(t)
Structural absence Reported as an explicit gap; never imputed silently

C.6 Aggregation Modes (Secondary Constructions Only)

Aggregation is performed only to produce secondary scalar constructions. The primary output is \mathbf{R}(t). Three aggregation modes are computed in parallel:

\mathcal{A}_{\text{geo}}(\mathbf{R},\mathbf{w}) = \exp\!\left(\sum_{i} w_i \ln(1+R_i)\right)-1
\tag{C.7}

\mathcal{A}_{\text{conc}}(\mathbf{R},\mathbf{w}) = \left(\sum_i w_i R_i^{\rho}\right)^{1/\rho},\quad \rho \in (0,1)
\tag{C.8}

\mathcal{A}_{\text{lin}}(\mathbf{R},\mathbf{w}) = \sum_i w_i R_i
\tag{C.9}

Rank stability across \{\mathcal{A}_{\text{geo}}, \mathcal{A}_{\text{conc}}, \mathcal{A}_{\text{lin}}\} and across weight perturbations \mathbf{w} \sim \text{Dirichlet}(\mathbf{1}) is reported as the primary robustness statistic.

C.7 Sensitivity Design

Variance-based sensitivity on the scalar constructions uses Sobol' indices

S_i = \frac{\mathbb{V}_{\mathbf{w}, \tilde{\mathbf{x}}}\!\left[\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{A}\mid w_i\right]\right]}{\mathbb{V}\!\left[\mathcal{A}\right]},
\qquad
S_{ij} = \frac{\mathbb{V}\!\left[\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{A}\mid w_i,w_j\right]\right]}{\mathbb{V}\!\left[\mathcal{A}\right]} - S_i - S_j
\tag{C.10}

estimated by a declared quasi-Monte Carlo design with a fixed seed in the pre-registration. Morris elementary effects are used for screening when the input dimension exceeds a declared threshold.

C.8 Structural-Break Handling

Break candidates are detected by the same methods used as early-warning baselines (Appendix F.5). When a break is confirmed, the frozen reference window is not silently updated. Instead:

1. a revision proposal is filed;
2. the proposal is peer-reviewed;
3. if accepted, a new reference standard is issued with a new identifier;
4. both old and new series are published side by side for a transitional period.

C.9 Sensitivity of Functional Forms

For each sub-index whose functional form is a modelling choice rather than a definitional identity (notably R_{\text{inst}} = \exp(-\lambda \cdot \max(0, \tau_{\text{policy}} - \tau_{\text{propagation}}))), at least two alternative forms are carried as sensitivity cases:

R_{\text{inst}}^{\text{lin}} = \max\!\left(0,\ 1 - \lambda_{\text{lin}} \cdot \max(0, \tau_{\text{policy}} - \tau_{\text{propagation}})\right)
\tag{C.11}

R_{\text{inst}}^{\text{log}} = \frac{1}{1 + \exp\!\left(\lambda_{\text{log}} \cdot \left(\max(0, \tau_{\text{policy}} - \tau_{\text{propagation}}) - \tau_0\right)\right)}
\tag{C.12}

with \lambda_{\text{lin}}, \lambda_{\text{log}}, \tau_0 calibrated on the same historical lag distributions as \lambda. The rank correlation across forms is reported.

