Appendix D — Coupling Estimation Architecture (Design Specification)

D.1 Purpose

To specify the tiered estimator for the directed coupling matrix \mathbf{D}(t), the preprocessing chain, the surrogate and bootstrap design, the identifiability conditions, and the uncertainty-reporting contract — as a blueprint, not as trained code.

D.2 Preprocessing Chain (Architectural Order)

1. Alignment to a common time grid \Delta t (declared per study).
2. Unit and scale normalization per Appendix C.
3. Stationarity screening: ADF and KPSS as dual tests; disagreement flags the series for differencing or detrending.
4. Detrending: locally weighted regression with a declared bandwidth, or first differencing, per series.
5. Outlier treatment: winsorization at a declared percentile, with an outlier log retained.
6. Embedding selection: false-nearest-neighbour and/or autocorrelation decay for \tau_e; Cao's method for k.
7. Window definition: W chosen so that W \geq W_{\min} (D.6).

D.3 Tiered Estimator Assignment

Network size Primary estimator Secondary estimator Rationale
\leq 30 nodes Mutual information (lagged) and Granger causality Partial cross-correlation Empirically accurate and computationally feasible for small networks
Priority pairs (contested directionality) Multivariate transfer entropy Conditional transfer entropy Highest accuracy; accept longer runtime
> 30 nodes / scaling Partial cross-correlation Regularized Granger (LASSO-VAR) Computational efficiency

D.4 Directionality and Lag Discipline

All estimators are applied on time-lagged data. Zero-lag association is retained only as a descriptive statistic and never interpreted as coupling. This is a hard rule:

\text{zero-lag statistic} \ \not\Rightarrow\ \text{coupling}.
\tag{D.1}

Transfer entropy is defined as

\text{TE}_{X\to Y} = \sum_{y_{t+1},\,y^{(k)}_t,\,x^{(l)}_t} p\!\left(y_{t+1}, y^{(k)}_t, x^{(l)}_t\right)\ \log \frac{p\!\left(y_{t+1}\mid y^{(k)}_t, x^{(l)}_t\right)}{p\!\left(y_{t+1}\mid y^{(k)}_t\right)}.
\tag{D.2}

Granger causality is defined through the nested VAR:

y_t = \sum_{p=1}^{P} a_p y_{t-p} + \sum_{q=1}^{Q} b_q x_{t-q} + \eta_t,
\tag{D.3}

and the null b_q = 0\ \forall q is tested. In the multivariate VAR representation

\mathbf{y}_t = \sum_{k=1}^{p} \mathbf{A}(k)\, \mathbf{y}_{t-k} + \boldsymbol{\eta}_t,
\qquad \boldsymbol{\eta}_t \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}),
\tag{D.4}

direct causality y_j \rightarrow y_i exists if A_{ij}(k) \neq 0 for at least one lag k = 1,\dots,p. Instantaneous couplings are not uniquely determined by \boldsymbol{\Sigma} alone; the Granger causality estimate remains well-defined because it depends only on the lagged coefficients and the residual covariance determinant, both of which are unique.

Partial cross-correlation is defined as the correlation between the residuals of X and Y after linear projection on the conditioning set Z.

D.5 Effective Transfer Entropy and Surrogates

Raw transfer entropy is biased upward by finite-sample effects. We adopt

\text{ETE}_{X\to Y} = \text{TE}_{X\to Y} - \big\langle \text{TE}_{X^{\text{surr}}\to Y} \big\rangle,
\tag{D.5}

with X^{\text{surr}} generated by the iterated amplitude-adjusted Fourier transform (IAAFT) or by block permutation when IAAFT is not appropriate for the series. B_{\text{surr}} is declared in advance; the default design value is B_{\text{surr}} = 200.

D.6 Identifiability Conditions

The estimation of directed coupling from observational time series is valid only under stated assumptions:

Assumption D.6.1 (Causal sufficiency). No latent confounder influences both X_i and X_j simultaneously. Where this assumption is violated, estimates are biased and the direction of bias is not recoverable without additional assumptions.

Assumption D.6.2 (Faithfulness). The observed conditional independencies in the data reflect the underlying causal structure; no accidental cancellation of paths occurs.

Assumption D.6.3 (Stationarity). The data-generating process is stable over the estimation window, or has been detrended under Appendix D.2 steps 3–4. Non-stationarity in the form of unit roots can produce spurious Granger causality; the ADF/KPSS dual screening is the primary guard.

Assumption D.6.4 (Correct embedding). The history lengths k, l and embedding delays \tau_e are correctly specified. Sensitivity to embedding misspecification is reported as part of the uncertainty contract (D.8).

Proposition D.6.5 (Sensitivity to unmeasured confounding). For a linear Gaussian system with latent confounder U influencing both X_i and X_j with coefficients \alpha, \beta respectively, the Granger causality estimate \widehat{\text{GC}}_{X_i \to X_j} satisfies

\widehat{\text{GC}}_{X_i \to X_j} \leq \text{GC}_{X_i \to X_j} + \alpha\beta \cdot \frac{\mathbb{V}[U]}{\mathbb{V}[X_i]},
\tag{D.6}

so that the bias is bounded by the product of confounder loadings and the variance ratio. A sensitivity analysis reports \widehat{\text{GC}} as a function of hypothetical \alpha\beta values.

D.7 Minimum-Series Design Constraint

Let W be the rolling window length and k, l the embedding dimensions. The estimator is declared valid only if

W \geq \max\!\left(W_{\min}^{\text{stat}},\ c \cdot (k+l) \cdot 2^{k+l}\right),
\tag{D.7}

where W_{\min}^{\text{stat}} is a stationarity-driven minimum and c is a declared constant. In practice, 10–20 bins and 500–1000 data points are generally sufficient to obtain a qualitatively robust estimate of the transfer entropy using time series data. However, the data requirements grow exponentially with the block length k+l and \max(k,l). If a domain's indicator series cannot satisfy (D.7), the domain is marked under-powered and excluded from headline coupling outputs; it may still enter the prior elicitation (Appendix E).

Fallback estimator. For under-powered domains, a reduced-order estimator is specified: bivariate Granger causality with P = Q = 1 and a bootstrap-percentile confidence interval. This fallback is reported with a distinct estimator identifier and is never merged into the headline \mathbf{D}(t).

D.8 Multiple Testing

Across the 6\times 6 = 36 directed pairs, the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure at level \alpha_{\text{FDR}} is applied (design default \alpha_{\text{FDR}} = 0.05). The full p-value matrix is published alongside the rejection set.

D.9 Uncertainty Contract

For each \widehat{D}_{ij}(t) the estimator publishes:

· point estimate;
· bootstrap percentile interval;
· surrogate-null p-value;
· estimator identity and hyperparameters;
· window W;
· effective sample size after preprocessing;
· embedding parameters k, l, \tau_e;
· sensitivity band under Assumption D.6.5.

This contract is binding for every release. A coupling estimate without this contract is not published.

D.10 Normalization to [0,1]

Effective transfer entropy is mapped to [0,1] by a monotone rank transform on the pooled surrogate-null distribution:

\widehat{D}_{ij} = F_{\text{null}}\!\left(\text{ETE}_{X_i\to X_j}\right),
\tag{D.8}

with F_{\text{null}} the empirical CDF of the surrogate-null ETE values. This yields \widehat{D}_{ij} interpretable as a calibrated directional coupling score.

D.11 Bayesian Update of the Prior

Given prior \mathbf{D}^{(0)} (Appendix E) and likelihood induced by \widehat{\mathbf{D}}(t) with declared uncertainty, the posterior is written formally as

p\!\left(\mathbf{D}\mid \widehat{\mathbf{D}}\right) \propto p\!\left(\widehat{\mathbf{D}}\mid \mathbf{D}\right)\, p\!\left(\mathbf{D}\mid \mathbf{D}^{(0)}\right),
\tag{D.9}

with the specific likelihood and prior family declared per study. \mathbf{D}^{(0)} is always identified as a prior and never presented as a measured matrix.

D.12 Benchmark Series Production

The production of open synthetic and historical multi-domain benchmark series is an explicit early deliverable of the Domain Nodes and Observatories. Annual recovery rates of known directed relationships on those benchmarks are published as a standing quality metric. Benchmarks include:

· synthetic VAR processes with known lag structure and varying noise;
· synthetic nonlinear systems with known coupling (coupled logistic maps, Kuramoto oscillators);
· historical multi-domain series with documented coupling (e.g., semiconductor capacity vs. compute deployment).

