Appendix E — Prior Coupling Matrix \mathbf{D}^{(0)}: Elicitation Protocol

E.1 Purpose

To specify how the initial prior coupling matrix is elicited, documented, and revised, without treating it as a measurement.

E.2 Elicitation Design

A structured Delphi process with three rounds:

1. Round 1. Each domain expert assigns a directional prior D^{(0)}_{ij} \in [0,1] for the six domains, with a short justification and a self-reported confidence.
2. Round 2. Anonymized distributions are returned; experts revise.
3. Round 3. Convergence criterion: interquartile range of each D^{(0)}_{ij} below a declared threshold. Non-converged entries are published as contested priors with a range.

E.3 Prior Matrix Structure

\mathbf{D}^{(0)} =
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & D^{(0)}_{12} & \cdots & D^{(0)}_{16}\\
D^{(0)}_{21} & 0 & \cdots & D^{(0)}_{26}\\
\vdots & & \ddots & \vdots\\
D^{(0)}_{61} & \cdots & & 0
\end{pmatrix}
\tag{E.1}

with zero diagonal (self-coupling is a separate research object) and asymmetry permitted.

E.4 Documentation Contract

For each entry, the release publishes:

· central value;
· interquartile range;
· expert count;
· justification text;
· convergence status;
· date of elicitation;
· revision identifier.

E.5 Revision Rule

\mathbf{D}^{(0)} is revised only by a documented, timestamped procedure. Revision triggers are: (a) new peer-reviewed evidence; (b) a Mission Cell output; (c) a confirmed structural break. Revision is not permitted to follow the direction of a desired policy outcome; the revision log records who proposed, who reviewed, and who approved.

E.6 Living-Object Status

\mathbf{D}^{(0)} is treated as a living object: it is updated through the documented triggers above, and its revision history is published alongside the posterior \widehat{\mathbf{D}}(t). No prior entry is ever silently overwritten.

