Appendix F — Early-Warning Architecture and Matched False-Alarm Protocol

F.1 Purpose

To specify (i) the four-channel early-warning architecture, (ii) the matched false-alarm protocol under which every detector is judged, (iii) the baseline detector definitions, and (iv) the pre-registration template for comparative validation.

F.2 Four-Channel Architecture (Candidate)

The architecture is a candidate, adapted from the Karimov–Alekberli (KA) framework. It consists of:

Channel Name Object
C1 Causal entropic response directional entropy change under perturbation
C2 Structural coupling covariance change in \mathbf{D}(t) structure
C3 Residual z-score deviation of a filtered residual
CB Correlation break change in pairwise correlation regime

The channels are fused into a scalar signal \text{fCER}(t) with persistent-alert detection. The KA authors report a mean detection rate of 85.0% with FPR = 0.00 across 196 events in seven domains, and C2 uniquely detecting 23/196 events. These are internal claims of the KA authors, derived from preprints, and are not treated as established performance in this dissertation. The exact figures are cited as author claims; the general requirement for matched false-alarm validation is grounded in the broader early-warning-signals literature, not in any single study.

F.3 Matched False-Alarm Protocol

Every detector — KA, WSNTTA-native, or baseline — is evaluated under a matched false-alarm protocol:

1. Alarm thresholds are set so that the trial false-alarm rate is at or below a declared budget \alpha_{\text{FA}} (design default \alpha_{\text{FA}} = 0.10).
2. Each result is scored with a label-permutation significance test.
3. Reported metrics: detection rate with Wilson 95% confidence interval, AUC, precision–recall, and cost-sensitive loss under a declared cost matrix.
4. If no detector reaches significance under this protocol, that null result is reported as a scientific finding, not suppressed.

This protocol is a methodological requirement independent of any single published study. Where the dissertation cites a specific multi-domain study documenting null results under matched false-alarm conditions, that citation is illustrative of the standard, not definitive of the outcome for any particular detector.

F.4 Data Corpora Design (Retrospective)

The pre-registered corpora for retrospective evaluation are:

· Generative AI adoption, 2022–2024.
· Industrial robot deployment in China, 2010–2020.
· Data-centre expansion, 2018–2026.

For each corpus, the pre-registration fixes (a) the indicators available at each date, (b) the declared transition onset, (c) the evaluation window, and (d) the frozen reference standard.

F.5 Baseline Detectors (Definitions)

CUSUM.

S_t = \max\!\left(0,\ S_{t-1} + (x_t - \mu_0 - \kappa)\right),\qquad \text{alarm if } S_t > h.
\tag{F.1}

EWMA.

z_t = \lambda x_t + (1-\lambda) z_{t-1},\qquad \text{alarm if } |z_t - \mu_0| > L\,\sigma_z.
\tag{F.2}

BOCPD. Bayesian online change-point detection over run-length posterior

p(r_t \mid x_{1:t}) \propto \sum_{r_{t-1}} p(x_t \mid r_{t-1}, x_{1:t-1})\, p(r_t \mid r_{t-1})\, p(r_{t-1}\mid x_{1:t-1}).
\tag{F.3}

Isolation Forest. Ensemble anomaly score

s(x, n) = 2^{-\frac{\mathbb{E}[h(x)]}{c(n)}},
\tag{F.4}

with h(x) path length and c(n) normalization.

Change-point methods. PELT, binary segmentation, and a Bayesian change-point baseline, with penalty selection declared in advance.

F.6 Pre-Registration Template for Comparative Validation

Field Content
Study identifier timestamped, hashed
Detectors list, with hyperparameter grids
Corpora list, with source and availability dates
Indicators codes from Appendix B
Normalization function identifiers from Appendix C
Coupling estimator tier and hyperparameters from Appendix D
False-alarm budget \alpha_{\text{FA}}
Success criterion \Delta\tau > 0, significance, FPR < 5% under matched protocol
Failure criterion \Delta\tau \leq 0 or unacceptable FPR
Replication at least one external group, independent of author and Scientific Core
Publication code, data, and protocol released together

F.7 Reporting Obligation

If the candidate architecture fails the matched false-alarm protocol on the pre-registered corpora, the failure is reported with the same prominence as a success would have been, and the architecture is either revised under a new pre-registration or retired.

