Appendix G — Federated Compute & Verification Nodes (FCVN): Reference Architecture

G.1 Purpose

To specify a two-tier architecture for proprietary telemetry exchange, its threat model, its differential-privacy accounting, its secure-computation layer, and its data schema — as design only.

G.2 Two-Tier Architecture

Tier 1 — Physically air-gapped testbeds. No network connectivity. Data ingress and egress by physical media under formal review. Used for autonomous-agent safety, closed-loop ecosystems, and robotic manipulation safety. This tier is deliberately not federated.

Tier 2 — Privacy-preserving federated telemetry exchange. Used for lower-risk proprietary data. Federated learning with joint differential privacy, stochastic privacy modules, secure multi-party computation, and trusted execution. This tier is federated by design; the phrase "air-gapped federated learning" is avoided because federated learning requires communication.

G.3 Threat Model

Adversary Capability Primary risk
Honest-but-curious server observes updates gradient leakage, property inference
Malicious client manipulates updates poisoning, backdoor
External observer observes traffic metadata leakage
Colluding clients combine updates reconstruction
Model inversion attacker queries model training-data recovery
Membership inference attacker queries model presence disclosure

Gradient leakage and property inference are treated as active, unsolved risks that mitigation reduces but does not eliminate.

G.4 Differential Privacy Accounting (Design)

Neighbourhood DP groups semantically related records into neighbourhood domains and permits privacy-budget reuse for continuous data. Gradient clipping with norm bound C and Gaussian noise with scale

\sigma = \frac{C\sqrt{2\ln(1.25/\delta)}}{\varepsilon}
\tag{G.1}

is applied. Composition is accounted by a declared accountant (Rényi DP or moments accountant), with a total budget (\varepsilon_{\text{tot}}, \delta_{\text{tot}}) fixed in advance.

Reported trade-off. The design declares that under strict privacy the accuracy cost is non-trivial; the specific accuracy–privacy curve is a study output, not a design assumption. The default design budget is declared, and any deviation is published with justification.

G.5 Stochastic Privacy Module (Mandatory Complement)

Differential privacy alone is not sufficient. Gradient Inversion attacks are a ubiquitous threat in federated learning as they exploit gradient leakage to reconstruct supposedly private training data. Common defense mechanisms such as Differential Privacy or stochastic Privacy Modules introduce randomness during gradient computation to prevent such attacks. However, if an attacker effectively mimics a client's stochastic gradient computation, the attacker can circumvent the defense and reconstruct clients' private training data. An extensive ablation study demonstrates that no individual defense provides sufficient privacy protection; only the combination of DP and a stochastic PM was sufficient to decrease the Attack Success Rate from 100% to 0%, and this combination consistently achieves the best trade-off between privacy and model utility.

The FCVN therefore mandates:

\text{Defense} = \text{DP} \oplus \text{SPM}
\tag{G.2}

where SPM is a stochastic privacy module whose noise scale \rho_{\text{PM}} is declared in the manifest and whose composition with DP is accounted under the same accountant. A submission employing DP alone, or SPM alone, is rejected at the schema-validation stage.

G.6 Secure Computation Layer

· SMPC. Additive secret sharing for aggregation; threshold t of n declared.
· TEE. Attested enclaves for sensitive computation; attestation chain published.
· Cryptographic attestation. Every measurement, computation, and audit record is hashed and signed; the chain is published as a transparency log.

G.7 Evaluation-Metric Rule

No participating entity chooses its own evaluation metric. All metrics are pre-registered in a public protocol before any data collection. Any deviation is logged and published.

