Appendix I — Governance, Funding Independence, and Conflict-of-Interest Instruments

I.1 Purpose

To specify the funding-concentration metric, the disclosure instruments, the phased independence schedule, and the conflict-of-interest register.

I.2 Funding Concentration Metric

\text{HHI} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} s_i^2,\qquad s_i = \frac{\text{contribution}_i}{\text{total contributions}}.
\tag{I.1}

Design thresholds:

Phase Years Donor cap HHI target Disclosure threshold
Founding 1–3 not enforced published only all contributions above 5%
Consolidation 4–6 15% declining all contributions above 2%
Mature 7+ 15% < 0.15 all contributions

I.3 Disclosure Template

For every contribution the register publishes: donor identity (where legally permitted), amount, date, purpose restriction (if any), and whether the restriction touches scientific themes. Any theme-restricted donation is refused or published as a refusal.

I.4 Governance Firewall Rules

1. Donors cannot vote on scientific priorities.
2. Donors cannot nominate Scientific Directors.
3. Dual membership (donor governance + scientific governance) is prohibited.
4. Term limits apply to all scientific governance roles.
5. All budgets and flows are published annually.
6. Any breach triggers an external review with published findings.
7. The first external review of the Scientific Core is scheduled before the end of the founding phase.

I.5 Conflict-of-Interest Register

Each participant declares: employment, consulting, equity, board seats, grants, and any relationship with entities that could benefit from a particular finding. The register is published; undeclared interests are grounds for removal from a given decision.

I.6 Precedent Notes (Design Rationale)

The African Economic Research Consortium model — diversified donors, no earmarking, a strong secretariat — is used as a design reference for scientific independence under multiple funders. AERC's control over the scientific agenda has given its members a sense of ownership and kept them coming back, and the diversified nature and periodic turnover of the Advisory Committee membership dispelled concerns that any one individual might be dominating it. Large public endowments illustrate long-horizon funding but do not by themselves establish independence from political capture; they are therefore treated as a funding source, not a governance model. The Japanese University Endowment Fund, for example, is cited as a funding-source precedent only.

I.7 Institutional Feasibility

The governance design is a normative proposal. Its political-economic feasibility depends on whether states, firms, and research institutions perceive participation as net-beneficial. The framework's feasibility analysis proceeds by analogy to existing multi-stakeholder scientific consortia (AERC, CERN, IPCC) and by explicit identification of incentive-compatibility conditions: (i) reputational returns to contributors, (ii) reciprocal access to aggregate benchmarks, (iii) firewall guarantees that no single funder captures the agenda. These conditions are stated as hypotheses to be tested during the founding phase, not as established facts.

