Appendix L — Uncertainty and Sensitivity Analysis Design

L.1 Purpose

To specify how uncertainty propagates from indicators to state vector to scalar constructions, as a design.

L.2 Uncertainty Sources

Source Model
Measurement error declared \epsilon_I per indicator
Missing data imputation distribution
Normalization parameter posterior over \mu, s, x_{\text{ref}}
Coupling estimate bootstrap and surrogate distributions
Aggregation weights Dirichlet prior
Structural breaks break-date distribution
Estimator choice ensemble over tiers
Functional-form choice ensemble over \{\exp, \text{lin}, \text{log}\}

L.3 Propagation Design

Monte Carlo propagation with N_{\text{MC}} draws and a fixed seed in the pre-registration. For each draw m, compute \mathbf{X}^{(m)}(t), \Omega^{(m)}(t), and the scalar constructions. Report:

· median and percentile intervals;
· variance decomposition by source;
· tail-risk summaries.

L.4 Sensitivity Design

Variance-based Sobol' indices (C.10) for scalar constructions, and Morris elementary effects for screening when the input dimension is large. Screening precedes variance-based analysis to reduce cost.

L.5 Reporting Contract

Every published scalar is accompanied by: the vector it was derived from, the aggregation mode, the weight vector, the sensitivity indices, and the uncertainty bands. A scalar without this contract is not published.

