Appendix P — Closing Specification Note

P.1 Status of These Appendices

These appendices specify design, protocol, schema, and reference architecture. They do not report executed experiments, trained models, or measured results. They are binding on future implementation in the sense that any future implementation that departs from them must file a versioned amendment under Appendix J and log it under Appendix K.

P.2 Consistency Obligations

The following consistency obligations hold across all appendices:

1. Every indicator in Appendix B has a normalization in Appendix C.
2. Every indicator in Appendix B has an uncertainty model \epsilon_I propagated through Appendix L.
3. Every coupling release satisfies the uncertainty contract of Appendix D.9.
4. Every early-warning claim satisfies the matched false-alarm protocol of Appendix F.3.
5. Every privacy claim satisfies the accounting of Appendix G.4 and the DP ⊕ SPM mandate of Appendix G.5.
6. Every scalar satisfies the reporting contract of Appendix L.5.
7. Every validation satisfies the pre-registration of Appendix J.2.
8. Every failure is recorded in Appendix K.
9. Every departure is documented in the revision log.

P.3 The Foundational Constraint

\boxed{\ \text{No Single Discipline Can Model the Transition Alone}\ }

\boxed{\ \text{Civilizational Questions} \Rightarrow \text{Cross-Domain Questions}\ }

P.4 Closing

These appendices are the operational skeleton of the framework developed in the main text. They are designed so that, when the moment comes to build rather than to specify, the builder inherits constraints that protect the science from the pressures that have historically distorted it: hindsight, metric capture, funding concentration, privacy erosion, and the quiet conversion of evidence into authority. The Network does not own the future. These appendices exist so that, when the future becomes infrastructure, it will at least have been observed by instruments that were declared in advance, tested honestly, and permitted to fail.

